### 13.8 Passwords from Better Auth


You **cannot** unhash existing passwords (one-way hash). But you **don't need to** — write a custom Django password hasher that verifies the existing Better Auth scrypt format. On next login per user, Django auto-rehashes to its default Argon2.


```python
# myapp/hashers.py
from django.contrib.auth.hashers import BasePasswordHasher
import hashlib, base64


class BetterAuthScryptHasher(BasePasswordHasher):
   algorithm = "better_auth_scrypt"


   def verify(self, password: str, encoded: str) -> bool:
       _, n, r, p, salt_b64, hash_b64 = encoded.split(":")  # adjust to actual BA format
       salt = base64.b64decode(salt_b64)
       expected = base64.b64decode(hash_b64)
       computed = hashlib.scrypt(
           password.encode("utf-8"), salt=salt,
           n=int(n), r=int(r), p=int(p),
           dklen=len(expected),
       )
       return computed == expected


   def encode(self, password, salt):
       raise NotImplementedError("legacy hasher — read-only")
```


```python
# settings.py
PASSWORD_HASHERS = [
   "django.contrib.auth.hashers.Argon2PasswordHasher",  # default for new
   "myapp.hashers.BetterAuthScryptHasher",               # legacy for migration
]
```


> Confirm the exact Better Auth scrypt string format by inspecting a real row in your pg_dump (`account.password` column). Better Auth uses scrypt by default per their docs.


### 13.9 What pg_dump CAN'T migrate


| Data | Where it lives | How to handle |
|---|---|---|
| **Storage bucket files** | Supabase Storage (S3-like) — **but this project uses Cloudinary, not Supabase Storage** | ✅ N/A — Cloudinary URLs continue to work without migration |
| **Edge Functions code** | Supabase platform — not used by this project | ✅ N/A |
| **JWT secrets / API keys** | Supabase platform-level | Regenerate in new setup |
| **Realtime subscriptions** | runtime state | Re-establish automatically on connect |
| **Postgres extensions** | per-database | Install on target DB **before** pg_restore |


---


## 14. Deployment checklist for the new repo


When you deploy the Django port:


- [ ] **DB**: Run the SQL migration on the new Postgres (the `CREATE TYPE` / `CREATE TABLE` block from Section 7.1)
- [ ] **Settings**: `TIME_ZONE = 'Europe/Athens'`, `USE_TZ = True`
- [ ] **Env vars**: Set all `WORLDLINE_*` + `CRON_SECRET` + `SITE_URL`
- [ ] **Cardlink portal**: ⚠️ Set "Recurring notify URL" → `https://<new-domain>/webhooks/worldline/` (most-forgotten step)
- [ ] **Disable old Vercel cron** before enabling Celery beat — otherwise both will charge
- [ ] **Test mode**: `PAYMENTS_TEST_MODE=true` for cutover so only admins hit the new flow
- [ ] **Backfill**: Run SCRUM-28 + SCRUM-54 backfill SQL (Sections 7.3 + 7.6)
- [ ] **Better Auth password hasher**: implement `BetterAuthScryptHasher` so existing 350 users keep their passwords (Section 13.8)
- [ ] **Postgres extensions**: install whatever was enabled on Supabase (check Supabase Dashboard → Database → Extensions before migration)
- [ ] **Cloudinary**: leave env vars unchanged — URLs in the DB keep working
- [ ] Once stable, flip `PAYMENTS_TEST_MODE=false`


